  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{2}t)),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t+2})\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{2}t),\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t+2}}\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=2,$ we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =\u27e8-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\pi ),\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2+2}}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e80,\\frac{1}{4}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=2$ we calculate the magnitude of $v(2),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(2)| & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
